#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
incident_controller - the fire ground, the crew register, and the water ledger.

One of these runs for the whole incident. It is uav_bushfire's fire_spotter
with the thing that makes this scenario a *cooperation* bolted into the middle
of it: water is finite, and it only reaches a fire by being carried there in
stages by two different kinds of vehicle.

    dam  --->  scout tender  --->  drone  --->  fire

Every arrow is a physical rendezvous between two vehicles that had to drive or
fly to the same place, and every link but the first is finite. A drone holds
~tank_capacity litres, which at ~flow_rate is about ten seconds of bombing -
less than a big fire needs, on purpose, so no fire above about an 8 can be put
out by one drone on one tank. A tender holds ~tender_capacity, about six drone
loads. The dam is the only inexhaustible thing in the world, and it does not
move, so the tenders have to keep going back to it.

Why the ledger owns the water
-----------------------------
It could have gone in the vehicles - each one knows when it is bombing or
filling. It is here because a transfer has two parties and a shared quantity,
and the two parties are separate nodes: a drone that decided for itself that it
had taken 40 litres, and a tender that decided for itself that it had given 30,
would both be sincerely wrong and nothing would ever notice. The same reasoning
put the crew register here rather than in the drones - see "Who chooses" below.
Local decisions, central bookkeeping.

It also has to be here for a duller reason: this node is already the thing that
computes, every tick, which drones are on station over which fire. Bombing
*is* the water consumption, so metering it anywhere else would mean a second
node deriving the same fact a tenth of a second later.

What it owns
------------
A fixed pool of ~num_hotspots slots. A slot is either burning - it has a
position, a value and a Gazebo model in the world - or it is out, in which case
it has no model, is not on the board, and is waiting out ~extinguish_pause
before lighting somewhere else. The pool is fixed so the fire ground never
empties and the fleet never runs out of work, but the fires themselves come and
go: putting one out **deletes its model**, and the one that lights afterwards is
a new model, built to its own size. See _extinguish() for why that is safe here
when the naive version of it is not.

What a fire looks like
----------------------
Its intensity drives its geometry, not just its payout: _flame_sdf() builds a
model whose flame height, ember bed, smoke column and light radius all scale
with the number. A 10 is a bonfire that lights the trunks around it; a 1 is a
smoulder. Before this, every fire was the same fixed model and the only place
size appeared was an RViz marker - so the single most important number in the
scenario was invisible in the window you were actually watching.

What a fire is worth, and what it costs
---------------------------------------
Its intensity, 1 to 10, is one number doing two jobs:

  value   the fire pays `intensity`, split equally between every drone that
          chose it. Three drones on a 9 get 3 each.
  size    it takes `intensity * ~work_per_intensity` drone-seconds of
          on-station bombing. Every crew member overhead contributes one
          second of work per second, so a crew of n finishes in 1/n the time.

Neither half is interesting alone. Split-only payout makes joining somebody
else's fire strictly worse than taking your own, and nobody ever forms a crew.
Work-only makes crews free, and every drone piles onto the same fire. Together
they are a real decision with a real answer: joining halves your share but also
halves the time, so what a drone actually maximises is share per second, and
the fire worth crewing is the big one - which is what "bigger fires draw more
drones" means mechanically rather than as an assertion.

Who chooses
-----------
The drones do. This node used to compute a greedy nearest-first assignment
every tick and push it out; it no longer assigns anything. Every aircraft sees
the whole board on /bushfire/hotspots - position, value, work remaining, crew
size - picks the fire with the best share-per-second for itself, and says so on
/bushfire/claims. This node is the ledger: it accepts the claim, tracks the
crew, meters the work, pays out and re-ignites.

The reason for moving the decision is not tidiness. Nearest-first has exactly
one fire per drone by construction, so it cannot express a crew at all, and
"how many drones should this fire have" is not a question a distance metric
has an opinion about.

Commitment
----------
A claim is binding: while a drone holds a fire, its claims on anything else are
ignored, and it is released only when the job is done. This is the opposite of
the old behaviour, which recomputed the whole assignment every tick, and the
reason for the reversal is crews. A drone that leaves halfway through has cost
its crewmates the work it was supposed to contribute, so a coalition you can
walk out of is not worth joining, so no coalition forms.

Two escapes exist, both faults rather than choices. A drone that has not been
on station for ~commit_timeout seconds is released, because a crew waiting on
an aircraft that is stuck in a thicket or repeatedly respawning waits forever.
And a drone that has been rescued releases its own claim (see fire_suppressor),
since it has been teleported to its launch point and its crewmates should not
be diluted by an aircraft that is not there.

Where a crew stands
-------------------
Not on the fire. Two drones station-keeping on the same point is a collision -
measured in the treasure version, which had nothing stopping two drones being
sent to one target. So a crew of n is spread over a ring around the fire, at
~crew_separation between neighbours, each member
~crew_altitude_step higher than the last. The ring radius falls out of the
chord: r = separation / (2 sin(pi/n)). Both the ring and the altitude stagger
have to stay inside the suppression test, which is why ~collect_dist here is
wider than a single drone would need and ~suppress_altitude has room above
drop_altitude for the whole stack.

Suppression
-----------
Per crew member: horizontal distance from the *fire* within ~collect_dist AND
altitude inside the band [~min_drop_altitude, ~suppress_altitude]. The ceiling
is the inversion of the treasure rule: there, altitude was part of the target
and had to be matched; here the fire is on the ground and altitude is a limit
the aircraft has to get under. A drone cruising over the fire has not put it
out.

The floor is there because "under the ceiling" is also true of an aircraft
sitting on the ground, and a parked drone has not made a drop either. Without
it a fire lit near a launch point is scored by a drone that has not moved yet -
measured, two of thirty-one drops in a run were awarded at 0.18 m, four seconds
before anybody took off. The band is a cheap way to say "flying, and low",
which is the thing actually being tested.

Passing the test no longer *ends* the fire; it means the drone is bombing, and
the fire's work counter advances while it is. The fire ends when the counter
fills.

...and it only advances while the drone has water. That is the single line
where this package differs from the one it grew out of, and everything else
about the scouts exists to serve it. A dry drone on station over a fire is
holding a perfect hover and achieving nothing.

The three transfers
-------------------
All metered here, all the same shape - two vehicles close enough to each other,
and a rate:

  fire   <- drone    ~flow_rate litres per second of bombing. Not a transfer
                     anybody has to arrange; it is what suppression costs.
  drone  <- tender   the drone within ~refill_radius of the tender and between
                     ~refill_min_altitude and ~refill_altitude - hovering over
                     its deck - and the tender stopped, with water. Fills at
                     ~refill_rate.
  tender <- dam      the tender within ~dam_radius of the dam. Fills at
                     ~dam_rate, which is much faster than it dispenses: the
                     interesting queue in this scenario is meant to be at the
                     tender, not at the dam.

The altitude band on the middle one is the same trick as the suppression band,
and it is load-bearing for the same reason. Without a floor, a drone sitting on
the ground next to a tender refuels; with one, it has to be flying and low,
which is what hovering over a deck actually is.

A tender is only `ready` when it is stopped, and that is checked here from its
odometry rather than taken on trust from the scout, because the consequence of
getting it wrong is a drone descending onto a moving vehicle.

Publications
------------
  /bushfire/hotspots       uav_bushfire/HotspotArray       every fire (10 Hz)
  /bushfire/water          uav_scout_bushfire/WaterBoard   the supply chain
  /bushfire/markers        visualization_msgs/MarkerArray  RViz
  /<ns>/bushfire/target    uav_bushfire/Hotspot            that drone's job

Subscriptions
-------------
  /<ns>/ground_truth/state nav_msgs/Odometry               per ns in ~drones
  /<ns>/odom               nav_msgs/Odometry               per ns in ~tenders
  /bushfire/claims         uav_bushfire/FireClaim          the fleet's choices

Gazebo services used
--------------------
  /gazebo/spawn_sdf_model  /gazebo/delete_model
  /gazebo/set_model_state  /gazebo/get_world_properties

ROS Parameters (private, ~)
---------------------------
  ~drones             list  Namespaces in play, e.g. [uav_0, uav_1] (default [])
  ~tenders            list  Scout namespaces, e.g. [scout_0]        (default [])
  ~num_hotspots       int   Fires burning at once                   (default 5)
  ~world_half_size    float Half-width of the fire ground, m        (default 4.0)
  ~collect_dist       float Horizontal bombing radius, m            (default 1.00)
  ~suppress_altitude  float Must be at or below this, m             (default 1.45)
  ~min_drop_altitude  float ...and at or above this, so that a
                            parked aircraft scores nothing, m       (default 0.45)
  ~work_per_intensity float Drone-seconds of bombing per point of
                            intensity                               (default 1.2)
  ~max_crew           int   Most drones one fire will accept        (default 3)
  ~join_deadline      float A crewed fire stops accepting new
                            members once this fraction of its
                            work is done                            (default 0.7)
  ~crew_separation    float Distance between neighbouring crew
                            stations on the ring, m                 (default 1.00)
  ~max_ring           float Cap on the ring radius, m; must leave
                            the ring inside ~collect_dist           (default 0.75)
  ~drop_altitude      float Height the first crew member holds, m   (default 0.95)
  ~crew_altitude_step float Each further member stacks this much
                            higher, m                               (default 0.15)
  ~commit_timeout     float Release a drone that has not been on
                            station for this long, s; 0 disables    (default 120.0)

  Water
  -----
  ~tank_capacity      float A drone's load, litres                  (default 40.0)
  ~tender_capacity    float A scout's load, litres                  (default 240.0)
  ~flow_rate          float Litres per second while bombing         (default 4.0)
  ~refill_rate        float Litres per second, tender to drone      (default 20.0)
  ~dam_rate           float Litres per second, dam to tender        (default 60.0)
  ~refill_radius      float How close a drone must hover to a
                            tender to be taking water, m            (default 0.90)
  ~refill_altitude    float ...and no higher than this, m           (default 1.30)
  ~refill_min_altitude float ...nor lower, so a drone parked beside
                            a tender does not refuel, m             (default 0.45)
  ~tender_still       float A tender is `ready` below this speed,
                            m/s                                     (default 0.08)
  ~dam_x, ~dam_y      float Where the standing water is             (default 5.0, 5.0)
  ~dam_radius         float A tender this close to it is filling, m (default 1.60)
  ~serve_radius       float A drone this close to a tender is in
                            the queue for it, m                     (default 2.60)
  ~serve_timeout      float Give the slot up if the nominated
                            drone has not filled in this long, s    (default 25.0)
  (the docking ring the tenders queue on is water_tender's ~dock_radius: it is
   geometry a scout drives, not bookkeeping this node keeps)
  ~min_separation     float Keep fires this far apart, m            (default 2.5)
  ~extinguish_pause   float A suppressed slot stays out of the
                            world and off the board this long
                            before lighting elsewhere, s            (default 1.5)
  ~flame_scale        float Whole-model size multiplier, for
                            taste; intensity does the real work     (default 1.0)
  ~fire_light         bool  Give each fire a point light. The one
                            cue that lights anything else           (default true)
  ~flicker_hz         float Yaw the flames at this rate to make
                            them move; 0 disables                   (default 4.0)
  ~publish_hz         float Publish rate in Hz                      (default 10.0)
  ~reignite_period    float Move an untouched, uncrewed fire after
                            this long, s                            (default 120.0)
  ~obstacles          list  [[x, y, radius], ...] to avoid          (default [])
  ~obstacle_clearance float Extra margin around obstacles, m        (default 0.8)
"""

import math
import random
import threading

import rospy
from nav_msgs.msg import Odometry
from geometry_msgs.msg import Pose, Point
from std_msgs.msg import ColorRGBA
from visualization_msgs.msg import Marker, MarkerArray
from gazebo_msgs.msg import ModelState
from gazebo_msgs.srv import SpawnModel, SpawnModelRequest
from gazebo_msgs.srv import DeleteModel, DeleteModelRequest
from gazebo_msgs.srv import SetModelState, GetWorldProperties

from uav_bushfire.msg import Hotspot, HotspotArray, FireClaim
from uav_scout_bushfire.msg import Tank, WaterBoard


# A fire, built to size and with no collision at all.
#
# No collision because the aircraft is meant to fly down into this, and a fire
# that could be shouldered aside by a rotor would be a different thing
# entirely.
#
# Why it is generated rather than a constant
# ------------------------------------------
# A fire's intensity is what it is worth *and* how big it is, and until this
# was built the second half of that was invisible in Gazebo: every fire was
# the same fixed model, and the only place size showed up was the RViz marker.
# So the model is built per ignition, scaled by intensity - a 10 stands more
# than twice as tall as a 1 and throws light much further. The value a drone
# is weighing is now the thing you can see out of the window.
#
# Nothing here can be done by scaling a model that already exists.
# gazebo_msgs/ModelState carries a pose and a twist and no scale at all, so a
# fire that changes size has to be a new model - which is why this file's
# old move-it-never-replace-it rule had to be revisited. See _extinguish().
#
# Why it looks the way it does
# ----------------------------
# Gazebo Classic has no particle emitter and no cone primitive, so a flame has
# to be built out of boxes, cylinders and spheres. Three things do the work:
#
#   emissive     under Gazebo's default lighting an unlit orange cylinder
#                reads as an orange cylinder. Emissive is what makes it read
#                as something burning, and it is graded through each tongue -
#                deep red at the base, orange in the body, near-white at the
#                tip - because a flame's colour gradient is most of what the
#                eye uses to recognise one.
#   a real light a point light at the base is the single biggest cue, because
#                it is the only one that affects anything else: the ground,
#                the tree trunks and the underside of a drone hovering over
#                the fire all get lit by it. Emissive geometry illuminates
#                nothing. Its range scales with intensity too.
#   asymmetry    three tongues of different heights at irregular bearings.
#                A radially symmetric flame looks like a traffic cone, and -
#                more to the point - it cannot flicker: the spotter animates
#                these by yawing the model a few degrees at ~flicker_hz, and
#                yaw does nothing at all to a shape that is the same from
#                every side.
#
# The scorch disc sits 1 mm up rather than flat on the ground plane, because
# coplanar faces z-fight and a flickering black disc under every fire looks
# like a rendering fault rather than a burn scar.

# The dam: standing water the tenders draw from, and the only inexhaustible
# thing in the world.
#
# Visual only, plus a low kerb with collision so a scout that misjudges its
# approach is stopped by something rather than driving through the pond. The
# water surface sits 5 mm above the kerb top so the two do not z-fight, and it
# is deliberately a flat disc rather than anything clever: the point of this
# model is to be unmistakable from across the arena at a glance, because "why
# is that scout driving to the corner" is the first question the scenario
# raises for anyone watching it.
_DAM_SDF = """\
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="bushfire_dam">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="kerb">
      <pose>0 0 0.06 0 0 0</pose>
      <collision name="kerb_col">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%(kerb).3f</radius><length>0.12</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="kerb_vis">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%(kerb).3f</radius><length>0.12</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.28 0.26 0.24 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.42 0.39 0.35 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <link name="water">
      <pose>0 0 0.125 0 0 0</pose>
      <visual name="water_vis">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%(water).3f</radius><length>0.01</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.02 0.16 0.30 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.05 0.32 0.62 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.4 0.5 0.6 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>"""


_TONGUES = (  # bearing (rad), radial offset, height multiplier
    (0.4, 0.06, 1.00),
    (2.7, 0.11, 0.74),
    (4.6, 0.09, 0.55),
)


def _sphere(name, x, y, z, radius, colour, emissive):
    r, g, b = colour
    er, eg, eb = emissive
    return """\
    <link name="%s">
      <pose>%.4f %.4f %.4f 0 0 0</pose>
      <visual name="%s_vis">
        <geometry><sphere><radius>%.4f</radius></sphere></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>%.3f %.3f %.3f 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>%.3f %.3f %.3f 1</diffuse>
          <emissive>%.3f %.3f %.3f 1</emissive>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
""" % (name, x, y, z, name, radius, r * 0.9, g * 0.9, b * 0.9, r, g, b,
       er, eg, eb)


def _flame_sdf(intensity, scale=1.0, light=True):
    """The SDF for one fire, sized by its intensity.

    `scale` is a whole-model multiplier for tuning the look; intensity is what
    actually drives the size. The mapping is deliberately not linear in radius:
    a fire twice as valuable should look considerably more than twice as
    alarming, but a 10 also has to stay small enough that a crew of three can
    stand on a 0.58 m ring around it without being buried in it. 0.62 to 1.35
    over the 1-10 range keeps the tallest flame just under the height the
    lowest crew member holds at.
    """
    s = scale * (0.62 + 0.081 * (max(1.0, min(10.0, intensity)) - 1.0))

    parts = ["""\
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="bushfire_flame">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="scorch">
      <pose>0 0 0.001 0 0 0</pose>
      <visual name="scorch_vis">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%.4f</radius><length>0.002</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.03 0.02 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.10 0.06 0.04 1</diffuse>
          <emissive>0.05 0.02 0.0 1</emissive>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <link name="embers">
      <pose>0 0 %.4f 0 0 0</pose>
      <visual name="embers_vis">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%.4f</radius><length>%.4f</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.10 0.0 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.8 0.15 0.0 1</diffuse>
          <emissive>0.85 0.16 0.0 1</emissive>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
""" % (0.62 * s, 0.045 * s, 0.34 * s, 0.09 * s)]

    for i, (bearing, offset, tall) in enumerate(_TONGUES):
        ox = offset * s * math.cos(bearing)
        oy = offset * s * math.sin(bearing)
        # Base, body, tip. Radii shrink and colour climbs together, which is
        # what gives the tongue its taper without a cone primitive.
        parts.append(_sphere('t%d_base' % i, ox, oy, 0.17 * s * tall,
                             0.205 * s, (1.0, 0.22, 0.02), (0.95, 0.20, 0.0)))
        parts.append(_sphere('t%d_body' % i, ox * 1.5, oy * 1.5, 0.40 * s * tall,
                             0.150 * s, (1.0, 0.50, 0.04), (1.0, 0.48, 0.02)))
        parts.append(_sphere('t%d_tip' % i, ox * 2.1, oy * 2.1, 0.58 * s * tall,
                             0.088 * s, (1.0, 0.86, 0.42), (1.0, 0.82, 0.35)))

    # Smoke. Translucent and unlit, and taller over a fiercer fire - from a
    # distance the smoke column is what tells you where the big one is.
    parts.append("""\
    <link name="smoke">
      <pose>%.4f %.4f %.4f 0 0 0</pose>
      <visual name="smoke_vis">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>%.4f</radius><length>%.4f</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.24 0.24 0.26 0.30</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.34 0.34 0.37 0.30</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
""" % (0.05 * s, 0.03 * s, 0.95 * s + 0.55 * s * intensity * 0.1,
       0.15 * s, 1.0 * s + 0.9 * s * intensity * 0.1))

    if light:
        # The one element that lights anything else. Range grows with the
        # fire, so a 10 washes the trunks around it and a 1 barely marks the
        # ground. cast_shadows off: five shadow-casting point lights in a
        # stand of timber costs more frames than it is worth.
        parts.append("""\
    <link name="glow">
      <pose>0 0 %.4f 0 0 0</pose>
      <light name="fire_light" type="point">
        <diffuse>1.0 0.52 0.14 1</diffuse>
        <specular>0.25 0.10 0.02 1</specular>
        <attenuation>
          <range>%.3f</range>
          <constant>0.28</constant>
          <linear>0.42</linear>
          <quadratic>0.12</quadratic>
        </attenuation>
        <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      </light>
    </link>
""" % (0.30 * s, 1.6 + 0.34 * intensity))

    parts.append('  </model>\n</sdf>')
    return ''.join(parts)


class _Fire(object):
    """One slot in the pool. Burning, or out and waiting to re-ignite.

    `model_name` is None while the slot is out, and carries a fresh sequence
    number on every ignition. Both facts matter - see _extinguish().
    """

    def __init__(self, fire_id):
        self.id = fire_id
        self.seq = 0
        self.model_name = None
        self.x = 0.0
        self.y = 0.0
        self.intensity = 1.0
        self.work_total = 1.0
        self.work_done = 0.0
        self.burning = False
        self.relight_at = rospy.Time(0)
        self.ignited_at = rospy.Time(0)

    def work_left(self):
        return max(0.0, self.work_total - self.work_done)

    def to_msg(self, crew=0, station=None, suppressed=False, reward=0.0):
        h = Hotspot()
        h.id = self.id
        h.x = self.x
        h.y = self.y
        h.intensity = self.intensity
        h.work_total = self.work_total
        h.work_done = self.work_done
        h.crew = crew
        h.share = self.intensity / float(max(crew, 1))
        sx, sy, sz = station if station is not None else (self.x, self.y, 0.0)
        h.station_x = sx
        h.station_y = sy
        h.station_z = sz
        h.suppressed = suppressed
        h.reward = reward
        return h


class IncidentController(object):

    def __init__(self):
        rospy.init_node('incident_controller', anonymous=False)

        self._drones       = rospy.get_param('~drones', [])
        self._tenders      = rospy.get_param('~tenders', [])
        self._tank_cap     = rospy.get_param('~tank_capacity', 40.0)
        self._tender_cap   = rospy.get_param('~tender_capacity', 240.0)
        self._flow         = rospy.get_param('~flow_rate', 4.0)
        self._refill_rate  = rospy.get_param('~refill_rate', 20.0)
        self._dam_rate     = rospy.get_param('~dam_rate', 60.0)
        self._refill_rad   = rospy.get_param('~refill_radius', 0.90)
        self._refill_alt   = rospy.get_param('~refill_altitude', 1.30)
        self._refill_min   = rospy.get_param('~refill_min_altitude', 0.45)
        self._still        = rospy.get_param('~tender_still', 0.08)
        self._dam_x        = rospy.get_param('~dam_x', 5.0)
        self._dam_y        = rospy.get_param('~dam_y', 5.0)
        self._dam_rad      = rospy.get_param('~dam_radius', 1.60)
        self._serve_rad    = rospy.get_param('~serve_radius', 2.60)
        self._serve_to     = rospy.get_param('~serve_timeout', 25.0)
        self._n            = int(rospy.get_param('~num_hotspots', 5))
        self._half         = rospy.get_param('~world_half_size', 4.0)
        self._cdist        = rospy.get_param('~collect_dist', 1.00)
        self._suppress_alt = rospy.get_param('~suppress_altitude', 1.45)
        self._min_drop_alt = rospy.get_param('~min_drop_altitude', 0.45)
        self._work_per     = rospy.get_param('~work_per_intensity', 1.2)
        self._max_crew     = int(rospy.get_param('~max_crew', 3))
        self._join_dl      = rospy.get_param('~join_deadline', 0.7)
        self._crew_sep     = rospy.get_param('~crew_separation', 1.00)
        self._max_ring     = rospy.get_param('~max_ring', 0.75)
        self._drop_alt     = rospy.get_param('~drop_altitude', 0.95)
        self._alt_step     = rospy.get_param('~crew_altitude_step', 0.15)
        # Longer than uav_bushfire's 90 s, and the refill trip is why: a
        # committed drone that runs dry has to fly to a tender, wait for it to
        # stop if it is moving, fill, and fly back. That is legitimately off
        # station for a while, and timing it out would take a fire off a drone
        # for doing exactly what the scenario requires of it.
        self._commit_to    = rospy.get_param('~commit_timeout', 120.0)
        self._min_sep      = rospy.get_param('~min_separation', 2.5)
        self._pause        = rospy.get_param('~extinguish_pause', 1.5)
        self._flame_scale  = rospy.get_param('~flame_scale', 1.0)
        self._fire_light   = rospy.get_param('~fire_light', True)
        self._flicker_hz   = rospy.get_param('~flicker_hz', 4.0)
        pub_hz             = rospy.get_param('~publish_hz', 10.0)
        self._reignite     = rospy.get_param('~reignite_period', 120.0)
        self._obstacles    = rospy.get_param('~obstacles', [])
        self._clearance    = rospy.get_param('~obstacle_clearance', 0.8)

        if not self._drones:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] ~drones is empty - no claims will be taken')
        if not self._tenders:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] ~tenders is empty - nothing will refill '
                          'the drones, so the fleet gets one tank each and '
                          'then holds station over fires it cannot put out')

        # One tank of water burns this many seconds of bombing, and a fire of
        # this size needs more than that. Worth saying out loud at startup,
        # because it is the number that decides whether the scenario is a
        # cooperation or three drones that never need anybody.
        seconds = self._tank_cap / max(self._flow, 1e-6)
        solo = seconds / max(self._work_per, 1e-6)
        rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] one tank = %.1f s of bombing = a fire of '
                      'about %.1f; anything bigger needs a refill or a crew',
                      seconds, solo)

        # The tallest crew station has to stay under the ceiling, or the last
        # member of a full crew hovers above the band and contributes nothing
        # while believing it is working. Cheap to check once, invisible if it
        # is wrong.
        top = self._drop_alt + self._alt_step * (self._max_crew - 1)
        if top > self._suppress_alt:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] crew of %d tops out at %.2f m, above '
                          'suppress_altitude %.2f - the top station cannot '
                          'bomb; lower ~crew_altitude_step or raise the ceiling',
                          self._max_crew, top, self._suppress_alt)
        ring = (self._crew_sep / (2.0 * math.sin(math.pi / self._max_crew))
                if self._max_crew > 1 else 0.0)
        if min(ring, self._max_ring) > self._cdist:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] crew ring of %.2f m falls outside '
                          'collect_dist %.2f - crew members would be off '
                          'station by construction', ring, self._cdist)

        # Per-drone state, all keyed by namespace, so a drone that never
        # publishes odometry simply never gets paid rather than holding up the
        # others.
        self._pose = {}
        self._have_pose = {}
        self._speed = {}
        self._score = {}
        self._drops = {}
        self._committed = {}
        self._commit_at = {}
        self._working_at = {}
        self._target_pub = {}

        for ns in self._drones:
            self._pose[ns] = (0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
            self._have_pose[ns] = False
            self._score[ns] = 0.0
            self._drops[ns] = 0
            self._committed[ns] = None
            self._commit_at[ns] = rospy.Time(0)
            self._working_at[ns] = rospy.Time(0)
            self._target_pub[ns] = rospy.Publisher(
                '/%s/bushfire/target' % ns, Hotspot, queue_size=10)
            rospy.Subscriber('/%s/ground_truth/state' % ns, Odometry,
                             self._odom_cb, callback_args=ns)

        # The water ledger. Drones and tenders keyed the same way, because a
        # transfer between them is symmetric bookkeeping - one side goes down
        # by exactly what the other goes up by, and that is much easier to keep
        # true when both live in the same dict.
        self._litres = {}
        self._delivered = {}       # tenders: litres handed to drones
        self._served = {}          # tenders: refills completed
        self._dam_trips = {}
        self._still_since = {}     # tenders: when it last started standing
        self._serving = {}         # tender -> the one drone allowed to descend
        self._serve_at = {}        # tender -> when that slot was granted

        for ns in self._drones:
            self._litres[ns] = self._tank_cap

        for ns in self._tenders:
            self._pose[ns] = (0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
            self._have_pose[ns] = False
            self._litres[ns] = self._tender_cap
            self._delivered[ns] = 0.0
            self._served[ns] = 0
            self._dam_trips[ns] = 0
            self._speed[ns] = 0.0
            self._serving[ns] = None
            self._serve_at[ns] = rospy.Time(0)
            rospy.Subscriber('/%s/odom' % ns, Odometry,
                             self._tender_odom_cb, callback_args=ns)

        self._water_pub = rospy.Publisher(
            '/bushfire/water', WaterBoard, queue_size=5)

        # fire id -> list of namespaces, in join order. A list rather than a
        # set because the index *is* the station: member 0 keeps its slot on
        # the ring when member 2 leaves, so nobody is shuffled sideways at low
        # level by somebody else's arrival.
        self._crew = {}
        self._crew_lock = threading.Lock()

        self._hotspot_pub = rospy.Publisher(
            '/bushfire/hotspots', HotspotArray, queue_size=5)
        self._marker_pub = rospy.Publisher(
            '/bushfire/markers', MarkerArray, queue_size=5, latch=True)

        # Serialises the moves, which run on their own threads so a Gazebo
        # service call can never stall the publish timer.
        self._model_lock = threading.Lock()

        rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] waiting for Gazebo model services...')
        rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/spawn_sdf_model', timeout=60.0)
        rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/delete_model', timeout=60.0)
        rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/set_model_state', timeout=60.0)
        rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/get_world_properties', timeout=60.0)
        self._spawn_srv  = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/spawn_sdf_model', SpawnModel)
        self._delete_srv = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/delete_model', DeleteModel)
        self._move_srv   = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/set_model_state', SetModelState)
        self._world_srv  = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/get_world_properties',
                                              GetWorldProperties)

        self._purge_stale_models()
        self._spawn_dam()
        self._fires = [_Fire(i) for i in range(self._n)]
        for fire in self._fires:
            self._crew[fire.id] = []
            self._ignite(fire, first=True)

        self._publish_markers()

        # Subscribed last, so no claim arrives before the fire ground exists.
        rospy.Subscriber('/bushfire/claims', FireClaim, self._claim_cb)

        rospy.loginfo(
            '[INCIDENT] fire ground ready | %d fires | +-%.1f m | bomb within '
            '%.2f m and %.2f-%.2f m | %.1f drone-s per point | crews up to %d '
            '| fleet %s',
            self._n, self._half, self._cdist, self._min_drop_alt,
            self._suppress_alt, self._work_per, self._max_crew,
            ', '.join(self._drones) if self._drones else '(nobody)')
        for fire in self._fires:
            rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] fire %d burning at (%.2f, %.2f) worth %.0f, '
                          '%.1f drone-s of work',
                          fire.id, fire.x, fire.y, fire.intensity, fire.work_total)

        self._last_tick = rospy.Time(0)
        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / pub_hz), self._tick)
        if self._flicker_hz > 0.0:
            rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / self._flicker_hz),
                        self._flicker_loop)

    # ------------------------------------------------------- Gazebo models --
    def _purge_stale_models(self):
        """Delete every flame left behind by a previous run.

        By prefix rather than by name, because the names carry an ignition
        sequence now and a crashed run leaves behind whichever numbers it had
        reached. Asked about first rather than deleted blind: Gazebo logs an
        ERROR for the delete of a model that is not there, and a screenful of
        those at every startup is indistinguishable at a glance from the ones
        that mean something.
        """
        try:
            names = [n for n in self._world_srv().model_names
                     if n.startswith('bushfire_flame') or n == 'bushfire_dam']
        except Exception:
            return
        for name in names:
            req = DeleteModelRequest()
            req.model_name = name
            try:
                self._delete_srv(req)
            except Exception:
                pass
        if names:
            rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] cleared %d flame(s) from a previous run',
                          len(names))

    def _spawn_dam(self):
        """Put the dam in the world, once.

        Spawned rather than written into the world file so that ~dam_x/~dam_y
        stay the single authority on where the water is - the tenders drive to
        the number on the water board, and a model that disagreed with it would
        be a scout parked politely beside a pond it cannot draw from.
        """
        req = SpawnModelRequest()
        req.model_name = 'bushfire_dam'
        req.model_xml = _DAM_SDF % {'kerb': self._dam_rad * 0.62,
                                    'water': self._dam_rad * 0.56}
        req.reference_frame = 'world'
        req.initial_pose = Pose()
        req.initial_pose.position.x = self._dam_x
        req.initial_pose.position.y = self._dam_y
        req.initial_pose.orientation.w = 1.0
        try:
            resp = self._spawn_srv(req)
            if resp.success:
                rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] dam at (%.1f, %.1f), draw radius '
                              '%.1f m', self._dam_x, self._dam_y, self._dam_rad)
            else:
                rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] dam spawn failed: %s',
                              resp.status_message)
        except Exception as exc:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] dam spawn error: %s', exc)

    def _spawn_flame(self, fire):
        """Put this fire's model into the world, built to its intensity.

        The name carries an ignition sequence, so no name is ever spawned
        twice in a run - see _extinguish() for why that is the whole trick.
        """
        fire.seq += 1
        name = 'bushfire_flame_%d_%d' % (fire.id, fire.seq)

        pose = Pose()
        pose.position.x = fire.x
        pose.position.y = fire.y
        pose.position.z = 0.0
        pose.orientation.w = 1.0

        req = SpawnModelRequest()
        req.model_name      = name
        req.model_xml       = _flame_sdf(fire.intensity, self._flame_scale,
                                         self._fire_light)
        req.reference_frame = 'world'
        req.initial_pose    = pose
        try:
            resp = self._spawn_srv(req)
            if resp.success:
                fire.model_name = name
            else:
                rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] spawn of %s failed: %s',
                              name, resp.status_message)
        except Exception as exc:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] spawn service error: %s', exc)

    def _despawn_flame(self, name):
        """Take a flame out of the world. This is what "the fire went out"
        looks like from the viewer's side."""
        req = DeleteModelRequest()
        req.model_name = name
        try:
            resp = self._delete_srv(req)
            if not resp.success:
                rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] delete of %s failed: %s',
                              name, resp.status_message)
        except Exception as exc:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] delete service error: %s', exc)

    def _flicker(self, fire, yaw):
        """Yaw the flame a few degrees. Cheap animation, no respawn.

        set_model_state is still the right tool for this and only this: it has
        no insert/remove race to lose, it works on a <static> model, and moving
        a model that is already there is all a flicker is. It is also why the
        tongues are at irregular bearings - yawing a radially symmetric flame
        does nothing at all.
        """
        state = ModelState()
        state.model_name = fire.model_name
        state.reference_frame = 'world'
        state.pose.position.x = fire.x
        state.pose.position.y = fire.y
        state.pose.position.z = 0.0
        state.pose.orientation.z = math.sin(0.5 * yaw)
        state.pose.orientation.w = math.cos(0.5 * yaw)
        try:
            self._move_srv(state)
        except Exception:
            pass

    def _flicker_loop(self, _event):
        if self._flicker_hz <= 0.0:
            return
        with self._model_lock:
            for fire in self._fires:
                if fire.burning and fire.model_name:
                    self._flicker(fire, random.uniform(-0.35, 0.35))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ placement --
    def _clear_of_obstacles(self, x, y):
        for obs in self._obstacles:
            ox, oy, radius = obs[0], obs[1], obs[2]
            if math.hypot(x - ox, y - oy) < radius + self._clearance:
                return False
        return True

    def _clear_of_fires(self, x, y, ignore_id):
        for other in self._fires:
            if other.id == ignore_id or not other.burning:
                # A slot that is out is about to be lit somewhere else, so
                # where it used to burn says nothing about where this one may.
                continue
            if math.hypot(x - other.x, y - other.y) < self._min_sep:
                return False
        return True

    # -------------------------------------------------------- the lifecycle --
    def _extinguish(self, fire):
        """Put the fire out: it leaves the world, and the board, for a moment.

        This is the half of "suppressed" that used to be invisible. The slot
        was moved straight to its next position, so from the viewer's side a
        fire never went out - one blinked from under three drones to somewhere
        across the arena, which reads as a fire that moved rather than a fire
        that was beaten.

        It is also what makes a per-intensity model possible at all.
        gazebo_msgs/ModelState carries a pose and a twist and *no scale*, so a
        fire that is a different size has to be a different model, and this
        node's old rule was that a flame is moved and never replaced. That rule
        existed for a real reason - delete-then-spawn races itself, because
        Gazebo answers both calls immediately and defers the work to a world
        update, so a delete can arrive before the insertion it is chasing and
        be told "model does not exist" while the insertion goes ahead anyway.
        Measured in the treasure version: 33 failed deletes in four and a half
        minutes and a target with no model in the world at all.

        Two things remove that race rather than tolerate it. The name carries
        an ignition sequence, so no name is ever spawned twice and a delete can
        never be confused about which insertion it refers to; and the pause
        between them is ~extinguish_pause, not zero, so the delete has a second
        of world updates to itself before anything is spawned. What is left is
        a delete of a model that has been standing for at least the time it
        took a drone to fly over and bomb it.
        """
        fire.burning = False
        fire.work_done = fire.work_total
        fire.relight_at = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(self._pause)
        name, fire.model_name = fire.model_name, None
        if name:
            threading.Thread(target=self._despawn_locked, args=(name,),
                             daemon=True).start()

    def _despawn_locked(self, name):
        with self._model_lock:
            self._despawn_flame(name)

    def _spawn_locked(self, fire):
        with self._model_lock:
            self._spawn_flame(fire)

    def _ignite(self, fire, first=False):
        """Light this slot somewhere clear, and roll its value.

        Rejection sampling, capped. Fires are kept ~min_separation apart as
        well as clear of the trees, and the separation has to clear two *crews*
        now rather than two drones: a full crew occupies a ring of up to
        ~max_ring plus a rotor span, so fires closer together than that would
        have their crews flying through each other.
        """
        for _ in range(200):
            x = random.uniform(-self._half, self._half)
            y = random.uniform(-self._half, self._half)
            if (self._clear_of_obstacles(x, y) and
                    self._clear_of_fires(x, y, fire.id)):
                break
        else:
            rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] no clear ground for fire %d in 200 tries; '
                          'check ~obstacles, ~min_separation, ~world_half_size',
                          fire.id)
        fire.x = x
        fire.y = y
        fire.intensity = float(random.randint(1, 10))
        # The two lines that make a big fire big rather than merely valuable:
        # this one in the mechanics, and the model _spawn_flame builds from the
        # same number in the world.
        fire.work_total = max(0.1, fire.intensity * self._work_per)
        fire.work_done = 0.0
        fire.burning = True
        fire.ignited_at = rospy.Time.now()
        # The first round is spawned inline so the fire ground is complete
        # before anything is published; after that it goes on a thread, because
        # a Gazebo service call must never stall the tick that is metering
        # everybody else's work.
        if first:
            self._spawn_flame(fire)
        else:
            threading.Thread(target=self._spawn_locked, args=(fire,),
                             daemon=True).start()

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- stations --
    def _station(self, fire, index, crew_size):
        """Where crew member `index` of `crew_size` holds while bombing.

        A lone drone gets the fire itself. A crew is spread over a ring whose
        radius is set by the separation wanted between neighbours - the chord
        of a regular n-gon, r = sep / (2 sin(pi/n)) - and stacked in altitude
        on top of that, because a ring alone puts two aircraft at the same
        height with a tree's worth of gap and rotor wash to share.

        The ring is phased by fire id so that two nearby fires do not lay their
        crews out along the same bearing and put two stations next to each
        other.
        """
        if crew_size <= 1:
            return (fire.x, fire.y, self._drop_alt)
        radius = min(self._crew_sep / (2.0 * math.sin(math.pi / crew_size)),
                     self._max_ring)
        theta = 0.7 * fire.id + 2.0 * math.pi * index / float(crew_size)
        return (fire.x + radius * math.cos(theta),
                fire.y + radius * math.sin(theta),
                self._drop_alt + self._alt_step * index)

    # -------------------------------------------------------------- markers --
    def _publish_markers(self):
        """One MarkerArray for the whole fire ground, in the world frame.

        World rather than any drone's odom frame: every aircraft shares it, so
        one RViz shows the whole incident without a transform between the
        namespaces.
        """
        arr = MarkerArray()
        now = rospy.Time.now()

        for fire in self._fires:
            crew = len(self._crew.get(fire.id, ()))
            done = (fire.work_done / fire.work_total
                    if fire.work_total > 0.0 else 0.0)

            if not fire.burning:
                # Out. Take both markers down rather than leaving a ghost
                # flame in RViz over ground where nothing is burning - the
                # Gazebo model has gone, and the two views should agree.
                for ns in ('fire', 'fire_label'):
                    gone = Marker()
                    gone.header.frame_id = 'world'
                    gone.header.stamp = now
                    gone.ns = ns
                    gone.id = fire.id
                    gone.action = Marker.DELETE
                    arr.markers.append(gone)
                continue

            flame = Marker()
            flame.header.frame_id = 'world'
            flame.header.stamp = now
            flame.ns = 'fire'
            flame.id = fire.id
            flame.type = Marker.CYLINDER
            flame.action = Marker.ADD
            flame.pose.position = Point(fire.x, fire.y, 0.3)
            flame.pose.orientation.w = 1.0
            # Fierce fires are visibly bigger here. The Gazebo model is one
            # fixed size, because it is moved rather than respawned and scaling
            # it would mean going back to respawning; a marker is free to
            # scale, so this is where intensity gets shown. Height shrinks as
            # the work is done, so a fire being bombed is visibly going out.
            width = 0.35 + 0.05 * fire.intensity
            flame.scale.x = flame.scale.y = width
            flame.scale.z = 0.6 * max(0.15, 1.0 - done)
            flame.color = ColorRGBA(1.0, 0.30 + 0.03 * fire.intensity, 0.0, 0.85)
            flame.lifetime = rospy.Duration(0)
            arr.markers.append(flame)

            txt = Marker()
            txt.header.frame_id = 'world'
            txt.header.stamp = now
            txt.ns = 'fire_label'
            txt.id = fire.id
            txt.type = Marker.TEXT_VIEW_FACING
            txt.action = Marker.ADD
            txt.pose.position = Point(fire.x, fire.y, 1.15)
            txt.pose.orientation.w = 1.0
            txt.scale.z = 0.28
            txt.color = ColorRGBA(1.0, 0.85, 0.4, 1.0)
            # Value first, then the deal on it: who is on it and what each of
            # them will get. A bare intensity was enough when a fire was worth
            # the same to whoever reached it first.
            if crew:
                txt.text = '%d  x%d @%.1f  %d%%' % (
                    int(fire.intensity), crew,
                    fire.intensity / float(crew), int(100.0 * done))
            else:
                txt.text = '%d' % int(fire.intensity)
            txt.lifetime = rospy.Duration(0)
            arr.markers.append(txt)

        self._marker_pub.publish(arr)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ callbacks --
    def _odom_cb(self, msg, ns):
        p = msg.pose.pose.position
        self._pose[ns] = (p.x, p.y, p.z)
        self._have_pose[ns] = True

    def _tender_odom_cb(self, msg, ns):
        """A scout's pose and, just as important, whether it is moving.

        The speed is read here rather than reported by the scout, because what
        it gates is a drone descending to hover over this vehicle. A tender
        that says it is stopped and is not would be an aircraft coming down
        onto a moving target, so the claim is checked against the same
        odometry everything else in this node is judged by.
        """
        p = msg.pose.pose.position
        v = msg.twist.twist.linear
        self._pose[ns] = (p.x, p.y, p.z)
        self._speed[ns] = math.hypot(v.x, v.y)
        self._have_pose[ns] = True

    # --------------------------------------------------------------- water --
    def _tender_ready(self, ns):
        """Stopped, and with something to give."""
        return (self._have_pose[ns] and
                self._speed.get(ns, 0.0) <= self._still and
                self._litres[ns] > 0.05)

    def _transfer_water(self, dt, now):
        """Meter the two transfers that vehicles have to arrange between them.

        Bombing is metered with the work, in _tick, because it *is* the work.
        These two are the ones that need a rendezvous.

        Both are deliberately written as "take the smallest of what the giver
        has, what the taker can hold, and what the rate allows in dt", so no
        amount of clock jitter can create or destroy water. Litres are the one
        quantity in this package that two nodes both believe in, and an
        accounting slip in either direction is invisible until the numbers stop
        making sense an hour into a run.
        """
        # ---- tender <- dam ------------------------------------------------
        for ns in self._tenders:
            if not self._have_pose[ns]:
                continue
            x, y, _ = self._pose[ns]
            if math.hypot(x - self._dam_x, y - self._dam_y) > self._dam_rad:
                continue
            room = self._tender_cap - self._litres[ns]
            if room <= 0.01:
                continue
            was_empty = self._litres[ns] < 0.05
            self._litres[ns] = min(self._tender_cap,
                                   self._litres[ns] + self._dam_rate * dt)
            if was_empty:
                self._dam_trips[ns] += 1
                rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] %s is drawing at the dam (trip #%d)',
                              ns, self._dam_trips[ns])

        # ---- drone <- tender ----------------------------------------------
        # One drone at a time, per tender. This is a queue rather than a free
        # for all, and the reason is collisions rather than plumbing: a tender
        # is a single point, so every drone that wanted water flew to that
        # point and station-kept on it. Measured on the first run with two
        # aircraft queueing - 0.20 m apart, one climbing away full while the
        # next descended into the same column, with rotor tips 0.396 m out.
        #
        # So the tender nominates one drone, everybody else holds off at their
        # own bearing (see _hold_point in fire_suppressor), and the nomination
        # is published on the water board so they all know whose turn it is.
        self._pick_served(now)

        for tns in self._tenders:
            ns = self._serving.get(tns)
            if ns is None:
                continue
            room = self._tank_cap - self._litres[ns]
            dx, dy, dz = self._pose[ns]
            if room <= 0.01 or not (self._refill_min <= dz <= self._refill_alt):
                # Nominated but not in position yet - it is still on its way
                # down. Hold the slot for it rather than giving it away.
                continue
            tx, ty, _ = self._pose[tns]
            if math.hypot(dx - tx, dy - ty) > self._refill_rad:
                continue

            took = min(self._refill_rate * dt, room, self._litres[tns])
            if took <= 0.0:
                continue
            was_dry = self._litres[ns] < 0.05
            self._litres[ns] += took
            self._litres[tns] -= took
            self._delivered[tns] += took
            if was_dry:
                self._served[tns] += 1
                rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] %s is taking water from %s '
                              '(%s has %.0f L left, %d refills given)',
                              ns, tns, tns, self._litres[tns],
                              self._served[tns])

    def _pick_served(self, now):
        """Decide which drone each tender is currently serving.

        Sticky: a drone keeps the slot until it is full, leaves the area, or
        runs out the safety timeout. Sticky matters more than picking well -
        a nomination that changed every tick would have two aircraft alternately
        descending and climbing in the same column, which is the failure this
        whole mechanism exists to remove.

        Candidacy is horizontal proximity within ~serve_radius, *not* being in
        the refill altitude band. It has to be: a drone only enters the band by
        descending, and it only descends once nominated, so judging candidacy
        by the band would mean nobody was ever nominated and no drone ever
        refuelled.
        """
        for tns in self._tenders:
            if not self._have_pose[tns]:
                self._serving[tns] = None
                continue
            tx, ty, _ = self._pose[tns]

            cur = self._serving.get(tns)
            if cur is not None:
                dx, dy, _ = self._pose[cur]
                near = math.hypot(dx - tx, dy - ty) <= self._serve_rad
                full = self._litres[cur] >= self._tank_cap - 0.01
                stale = (now - self._serve_at[tns]).to_sec() > self._serve_to
                if near and not full and not stale:
                    continue
                if stale:
                    rospy.logwarn('[INCIDENT] %s held %s\'s slot for %.0f s '
                                  'without filling - giving it up',
                                  cur, tns, self._serve_to)
                self._serving[tns] = None

            if not self._tender_ready(tns):
                continue

            best, best_d = None, self._serve_rad
            for ns in self._drones:
                if not self._have_pose[ns]:
                    continue
                if self._litres[ns] >= self._tank_cap - 0.01:
                    continue
                dx, dy, _ = self._pose[ns]
                d = math.hypot(dx - tx, dy - ty)
                if d <= best_d:
                    best, best_d = ns, d
            if best is not None:
                self._serving[tns] = best
                self._serve_at[tns] = now

    def _water_board(self, stamp):
        board = WaterBoard()
        board.header.stamp = stamp
        board.header.frame_id = 'world'
        board.dam_x = self._dam_x
        board.dam_y = self._dam_y
        board.dam_radius = self._dam_rad

        for ns in self._drones:
            t = Tank()
            t.vehicle = ns
            t.tender = False
            t.litres = self._litres[ns]
            t.capacity = self._tank_cap
            t.x, t.y, _ = self._pose[ns]
            t.ready = False
            board.tanks.append(t)

        for ns in self._tenders:
            t = Tank()
            t.vehicle = ns
            t.tender = True
            t.litres = self._litres[ns]
            t.capacity = self._tender_cap
            t.x, t.y, _ = self._pose[ns]
            t.ready = self._tender_ready(ns)
            t.serving = self._serving.get(ns) or ''
            board.tanks.append(t)

        return board

    def _claim_cb(self, msg):
        """A drone announcing what it has chosen. The ledger's only input.

        Everything here is a rejection rule, and each one is load-bearing:

          unknown drone   nothing else in the run would notice, and a typo in
                          ~drones would otherwise show up as one aircraft that
                          silently never scores.
          already holding a fire   the commitment rule. Ignored rather than
                          answered, because the drone is already being sent
                          its real assignment ten times a second and that is
                          the answer.
          crew is full    the ring only has so many stations.
          crew is closed  the fire is more than ~join_deadline of the way out.
                          Equal shares plus shared labour has one degenerate
                          corner and this is it: `work_left / n` collapses as a
                          job finishes, so a fire at 95% is the best-looking
                          thing on the board and joining it pays a full share
                          for turning up after it is over. Measured on the
                          first run: a drone bombed a 7 alone for fourteen
                          seconds and was joined at 0.3 drone-seconds
                          remaining by both others, keeping 2.33 of it. The
                          deadline only applies to a fire that already has a
                          crew - taking over an abandoned job at 90% is fine,
                          because there is nobody to take it from.

        A refused claimant is not told. It keeps claiming until something is
        accepted and re-picks on its own timeout, which is the same code path
        as a lost message.
        """
        ns = msg.drone
        if ns not in self._committed:
            rospy.logwarn_throttle(10.0, '[INCIDENT] claim from unknown drone %r '
                                   '- is it in ~drones?', ns)
            return

        with self._crew_lock:
            held = self._committed[ns]

            if msg.release:
                if held is None:
                    return
                self._release(ns, held, 'released by the aircraft')
                return

            if held is not None:
                if held != msg.fire_id:
                    rospy.loginfo_throttle(
                        5.0, '[INCIDENT] %s wants fire %d but still owes fire '
                        '%d - claim ignored', ns, msg.fire_id, held)
                return

            fire = self._fire_by_id(msg.fire_id)
            if fire is None:
                return
            if not fire.burning:
                # It went out between the board being published and this claim
                # arriving. The slot is not on the board while it is out, so
                # the drone will pick again from the next one.
                return
            crew = self._crew[fire.id]
            if len(crew) >= self._max_crew:
                rospy.loginfo_throttle(
                    5.0, '[INCIDENT] %s claimed fire %d but its crew is full '
                    '(%d)', ns, fire.id, self._max_crew)
                return
            if (crew and fire.work_total > 0.0 and
                    fire.work_done / fire.work_total > self._join_dl):
                rospy.loginfo_throttle(
                    5.0, '[INCIDENT] %s claimed fire %d but it is %.0f%% out - '
                    'the crew is closed', ns, fire.id,
                    100.0 * fire.work_done / fire.work_total)
                return

            crew.append(ns)
            self._committed[ns] = fire.id
            self._commit_at[ns] = rospy.Time.now()
            self._working_at[ns] = rospy.Time(0)
            rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] %s joins fire %d (worth %.0f, %.1f drone-s '
                          'left) - crew of %d, %.2f each',
                          ns, fire.id, fire.intensity, fire.work_left(),
                          len(crew), fire.intensity / float(len(crew)))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- registry --
    def _release(self, ns, fire_id, why):
        """Drop one drone out of one crew. Caller holds _crew_lock."""
        crew = self._crew.get(fire_id)
        if crew and ns in crew:
            crew.remove(ns)
        self._committed[ns] = None
        rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] %s off fire %d - %s (crew now %d)',
                      ns, fire_id, why, len(self._crew.get(fire_id, ())))

    def _fire_by_id(self, fire_id):
        for fire in self._fires:
            if fire.id == fire_id:
                return fire
        return None

    def _on_station(self, ns, fire):
        """Is this drone actually bombing this fire right now?

        Measured against the *fire*, not the drone's ring station, and that is
        deliberate: a crew member holding a slightly sloppy station is still
        over the fire and still working, and making the test the station would
        mean the geometry this node invented for collision reasons could also
        silently stop the work.
        """
        if not self._have_pose[ns]:
            return False
        dx, dy, dz = self._pose[ns]
        return (math.hypot(fire.x - dx, fire.y - dy) <= self._cdist and
                self._min_drop_alt <= dz <= self._suppress_alt)

    # ----------------------------------------------------------------- tick --
    def _tick(self, _event):
        now = rospy.Time.now()
        dt = 0.0 if self._last_tick == rospy.Time(0) else (now - self._last_tick).to_sec()
        self._last_tick = now
        # A stalled simulator or a paused physics step must not hand a fire a
        # second of free work, and sim time can go backwards on a reset.
        dt = max(0.0, min(dt, 1.0))

        moved = False
        outbox = []

        # Outside the crew lock: the transfers touch tanks and poses, neither
        # of which the claim path looks at.
        self._transfer_water(dt, now)

        with self._crew_lock:
            # -------- slots that are out come back -----------------------
            # A suppressed fire leaves the world and the board for
            # ~extinguish_pause, then this slot lights somewhere else. The gap
            # is what makes a drop look like a drop rather than a fire
            # teleporting across the arena, and it is also the window that
            # makes deleting and respawning the model safe - see _extinguish().
            for fire in self._fires:
                if not fire.burning and now >= fire.relight_at:
                    self._ignite(fire)
                    rospy.loginfo(
                        '[INCIDENT] fire %d re-ignited at (%.2f, %.2f) worth '
                        '%.0f, %.1f drone-s', fire.id, fire.x, fire.y,
                        fire.intensity, fire.work_total)
                    moved = True

            # -------- the work, and the payout ---------------------------
            for fire in self._fires:
                crew = self._crew[fire.id]
                if not crew or not fire.burning:
                    continue

                # On station AND with something in the tank. This is the line
                # the whole ground fleet exists to serve: a dry drone holding a
                # perfect hover over a fire achieves precisely nothing, and the
                # only way it gets wet again is another vehicle driving to it.
                on_station = [ns for ns in crew if self._on_station(ns, fire)]
                working = [ns for ns in on_station if self._litres[ns] > 0.0]

                for ns in on_station:
                    # Being over the fire is what proves the drone has not
                    # abandoned the job, so the commitment clock is reset by
                    # presence rather than by useful work. A drone that is
                    # there and dry is waiting for water, not shirking.
                    self._working_at[ns] = now

                for ns in working:
                    # Bombing is the water going somewhere. Metered against the
                    # same dt as the work so the two can never disagree about
                    # how long a drone was on station.
                    self._litres[ns] = max(0.0, self._litres[ns] - self._flow * dt)
                    if self._litres[ns] <= 0.0:
                        rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] %s is dry over fire %d '
                                      '(%.0f%% out) - needs a tender',
                                      ns, fire.id,
                                      100.0 * fire.work_done / fire.work_total)

                if working:
                    # n drones, n seconds of work per second. This is the whole
                    # reason a crew is worth joining.
                    fire.work_done += len(working) * dt

                if fire.work_done < fire.work_total:
                    continue

                # Out. Equal shares to everyone who chose it - including a
                # member that contributed nothing, which is what "shared
                # equally between the drones that chose it" means and is
                # exactly why the commitment has a timeout behind it.
                reward = fire.intensity / float(len(crew))
                for ns in list(crew):
                    self._score[ns] += reward
                    self._drops[ns] += 1
                    self._committed[ns] = None
                    outbox.append((ns, fire.to_msg(crew=len(crew),
                                                   suppressed=True,
                                                   reward=reward)))
                rospy.loginfo(
                    '[INCIDENT] fire %d (worth %.0f) OUT at (%.2f, %.2f) - crew '
                    'of %d took %.1f drone-s, %.2f each | %s',
                    fire.id, fire.intensity, fire.x, fire.y, len(crew),
                    fire.work_total, reward,
                    ' '.join('%s=%.1f' % (ns, self._score[ns]) for ns in crew))
                self._crew[fire.id] = []
                self._extinguish(fire)
                moved = True

            # -------- drones that are not turning up ---------------------
            # A commitment nothing can break is a crew that waits forever on an
            # aircraft wedged in a thicket or stuck in a respawn loop. This is
            # the only involuntary way out, and it is deliberately long: it is
            # a fault handler, not a way to change your mind.
            if self._commit_to > 0.0:
                for ns in self._drones:
                    fire_id = self._committed[ns]
                    if fire_id is None:
                        continue
                    last = max(self._commit_at[ns], self._working_at[ns])
                    if (now - last).to_sec() > self._commit_to:
                        self._release(ns, fire_id,
                                      'not on station for %.0f s' % self._commit_to)

            # -------- the slow backstop ----------------------------------
            # A fire nobody has chosen in a long time burns out on its own and
            # lights elsewhere. Uncrewed only: a fire with drones committed to
            # it is being flown to, and moving it under them would strand a
            # whole coalition rather than one drone.
            for fire in self._fires:
                if self._crew[fire.id] or not fire.burning:
                    continue
                if (self._reignite > 0.0 and
                        (now - fire.ignited_at).to_sec() > self._reignite):
                    rospy.loginfo('[INCIDENT] fire %d has burned unchosen for '
                                  '%.0f s - letting it burn out', fire.id,
                                  self._reignite)
                    self._extinguish(fire)
                    moved = True

            # -------- what everybody is doing next -----------------------
            for fire in self._fires:
                crew = self._crew[fire.id]
                for i, ns in enumerate(crew):
                    outbox.append((ns, fire.to_msg(
                        crew=len(crew),
                        station=self._station(fire, i, len(crew)))))

            # Burning fires only. A slot that is out is not somewhere a drone
            # can be sent, and leaving it on the board would have the fleet
            # bidding on the patch of ground where a fire used to be.
            board = HotspotArray()
            board.header.stamp = now
            board.header.frame_id = 'world'
            board.hotspots = [f.to_msg(crew=len(self._crew[f.id]))
                              for f in self._fires if f.burning]

        # Published outside the lock: a subscriber callback that claims in
        # response would otherwise deadlock against the ledger it is claiming
        # into.
        for ns, msg in outbox:
            self._target_pub[ns].publish(msg)
        self._hotspot_pub.publish(board)
        self._water_pub.publish(self._water_board(now))
        self._publish_markers()
        if moved:
            rospy.logdebug('[INCIDENT] fire ground changed')


def main():
    IncidentController()
    rospy.spin()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
